INSTALLATION AND SETUP
======================

Prerequisites
-------------

- Python 3 (required for index generation and deduplication)
- Web server (any of the following):
  - suckless quark (standalone binary from https://tools.suckless.org/quark/)
  - Docker (for the containerized quark image)
  - Python 3 http.server (standard library, zero extra installs)
- Optional: httrack (for mirroring external documentation sites)


1. Directory Setup
------------------

Create the target documentation folder:

    mkdir -p ~/localdocs/docs

Add your documentation content to ~/localdocs:
- Unpack code doc archives into their own subdirectories.
- Place standalone manuals, PDFs, and text files in ~/localdocs or ~/localdocs/docs.
- Mirror websites with httrack directly into ~/localdocs.


2. CLI Tool Installation
------------------------

Symlink the update tool into your local PATH:

    mkdir -p ~/.local/bin
    ln -sf "$(pwd)/localdocs-update" ~/.local/bin/localdocs-update

Ensure ~/.local/bin is present in your shell PATH.


3. Index Generation
-------------------

Rebuild the index pages (index.html and pages.html):

    localdocs-update

Or run the scripts individually on any directory:

    ./localdocs-dedupe /path/to/docs
    ./update.sh /path/to/docs


4. Hosting
----------

Choose any server method:

Method 1: Native suckless quark
Run quark directly pointing to the documentation directory:

    quark -l -h 0.0.0.0 -p 8000 -d ~/localdocs

Method 2: Universal runner (serve.sh)
Auto-detects available server backends or accepts flags:

    ./serve.sh ~/localdocs
    ./serve.sh --server python --port 8000 ~/localdocs
    ./serve.sh --host 0.0.0.0 --port 9000 ~/localdocs

Method 3: Containerized quark (Docker)
Builds a scratch container mapped to your host user ID:

    ./run-quark.sh ~/localdocs

Method 4: Python standard library
Requires no extra packages or containers:

    python3 -m http.server -b 127.0.0.1 -d ~/localdocs 8000


5. Editor Integration
---------------------

Access your documentation at http://127.0.0.1:8000/ (or your configured port).
Bind an editor shortcut to launch documentation URLs:

    xdg-open http://127.0.0.1:8000/
